/target

# Runtime artifacts of the stage lifecycle. `stage commit`, `stage verify`, and
# `prune` write the advisory-lock entry together with its owner record, the
# boundary snapshot, and the staged and parked siblings of both beside the marker
# they guard, and the lock entry and its record are deliberately never removed,
# so none of them may enter version control.
#
# Every name is derived from three coordinates — the marker that guards the
# artifact, the writer that created it, and the per-process nonce that keeps a
# recycled process id (or a second host sharing the id) from being read as the
# same writer — by the naming rules in `crates/rurqe-cli/src/cli/commands` and
# `crates/rurqe-cli/src/cli/commands/stage/swap`. A marker `checkpoint.toml` is
# guarded by `checkpoint.lock`, whose owner record is `checkpoint.lock.record`,
# and is snapshotted into the `<stem>-stage/` directory beside it. A staged
# sibling is `.{basename}.{pid}.{nonce}.{sequence}.tmp` and a parked copy is
# `.{basename}.{pid}.{nonce}.previous`, so the patterns below pin all three
# fields instead of letting one wildcard swallow the writer scope.
#
# The boundary pattern is anchored to the `-stage` suffix every boundary
# directory carries after the stem of its marker, so it describes the snapshot
# of whatever marker a project keeps — `checkpoint.toml` keeps
# `checkpoint-stage/`, `prod.toml` keeps `prod-stage/` — and it matches at any
# depth, because the marker may sit in a subdirectory such as `migrations/`.
# The remaining patterns name the artifacts of the conventional `checkpoint.toml`
# marker: a project that points the toolchain at a differently named marker
# ignores its artefacts by adding that stem alongside them.
*-stage/
checkpoint.lock
checkpoint.lock.record
.checkpoint.toml.*.*.*.tmp
.checkpoint-stage.*.*.*.tmp
.checkpoint.lock.record.*.*.*.tmp
.schema.lock.*.*.*.tmp
.checkpoint.toml.*.*.previous
.checkpoint-stage.*.*.previous
